EDITS = {
    "metaphysics/categories-of-nihilism/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-1", heading="Nihilisms differ by what they deny, so the label alone explains very little", paragraph="Existential nihilism denies objective meaning, moral nihilism denies objective moral facts, epistemological nihilism attacks the possibility of knowledge, and political nihilism rejects the legitimacy or value of political order. Accepting one does not logically require accepting the others: someone might deny cosmic purpose while defending knowledge and human-made ethical commitments. The useful question is therefore never merely ‘Is this nihilism?’ but ‘What is being denied, on what grounds, and what follows from that denial?’"),
    "metaphysics/dualismvsmaterialism/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-5", heading="A comparison table should separate evidence, explanation, and unresolved costs", paragraph="Dualism may seem to preserve the privacy and qualitative character of experience, yet it owes an account of how a nonphysical mind interacts with a physical brain. Materialism fits the causal success of neuroscience, yet it still faces hard questions about why physical processing is accompanied by experience at all. A fair comparison does not award victory for naming the other view’s mystery; it asks which view explains more with fewer unsupported commitments."),
    "metaphysics/establishing-the-spiritual/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-4", heading="A profound experience establishes its psychological reality before its metaphysical source", paragraph="Awe, unity, transcendence, and a sensed presence can be life-changing facts about a person’s experience. Moving from that fact to a spiritual cause requires an additional inference, because sleep loss, ritual, grief, meditation, substances, and neurological conditions can generate overlapping reports. Respecting the experience and testing its interpretation are compatible: the first concerns what was felt, while the second concerns what best explains it."),
    "miscellany/covid-19-science/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-1", heading="The pandemic tested science, institutions, communication, and public trust at once", paragraph="Rapid sequencing, vaccine development, and large collaborative studies displayed science’s capacity to learn under pressure. Conflicting early guidance, uneven data, political incentives, and confident messaging about uncertain evidence exposed weaknesses in the systems carrying science to the public. The lesson is not that changing advice discredits science; it is that uncertainty, revision, and the boundary between evidence and policy judgment must be communicated plainly."),
    "philosophical-inquiry/dangers-ideologies-of-emotion/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-2", heading="An ideology can exploit emotion without every emotional commitment being irrational", paragraph="Fear can magnify threats, belonging can discourage dissent, resentment can select convenient villains, and hope can make promised outcomes feel more probable than the evidence warrants. Yet emotion also identifies what matters and supplies motivation to act; a perfectly detached thinker would not thereby know which goals deserve pursuit. The safeguard is to let emotion disclose stakes while independent evidence tests descriptions, causes, and predictions."),
    "philosophical-inquiry/inscrutability-case-studies/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-2", heading="A promise explains nothing when every possible outcome is counted as fulfillment", paragraph="If recovery confirms prayer, deterioration reveals a hidden purpose, delay tests faith, and silence is itself an answer, no observation can count against the claim. Such flexibility protects consolation but destroys discriminatory power—the ability of evidence to favor one explanation over rivals. A testable claim must say, before the outcome, what would be more expected if it were true and what would be more expected if it were false."),
    "philosophical-inquiry/seeker-scenarios/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-5", heading="Good advice turns a dramatic life question into small, reversible tests", paragraph="A seeker need not decide between total conformity and an irreversible leap. Short courses, volunteer work, informational interviews, a reduced schedule, or a time-limited move can reveal whether imagined satisfaction survives contact with daily reality. This experimental approach respects longing while protecting finances, relationships, and future options from a decision made mainly under the pressure of urgency."),
    "philosophy-of-ai/feedback-loops/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-3", heading="Embodiment supplies consequences and constraints that text alone can conceal", paragraph="A robot that grasps a cup must reconcile vision, touch, force, timing, and the cost of dropping it; errors become concrete rather than merely verbal. That feedback can improve world models and practical reasoning, but a body does not automatically produce general intelligence or consciousness. What matters is the learning loop: diverse observation, purposeful action, measurable consequences, and correction that transfers beyond one task."),
    "philosophy-of-ai/human-reaction-to-ai/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-4", heading="AI adoption will track each field’s error costs, incentives, and sources of legitimacy", paragraph="Engineering can accept tools that are benchmarked against tolerances, while medicine and law demand traceability because errors directly harm people. Media rewards speed but risks destroying trust; politics and religion also protect authority, identity, and values that cannot be settled by predictive accuracy alone. Adoption will therefore be uneven even when the underlying tool is identical, because institutions disagree about what counts as a successful answer and who may give it."),
    "philosophy-of-ai/philosophy-of-ai-core-concepts/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-2", heading="The core concepts form a chain from performance to responsibility", paragraph="Capability asks what a system can do; agency asks whether its behavior is organized around goals; understanding asks what kind of grasp, if any, supports that behavior; alignment asks whether its objectives and effects fit human values. Responsibility then asks who is answerable—the developer, deployer, user, institution, or perhaps some future artificial agent. Keeping the links distinct prevents impressive performance from silently answering every deeper philosophical question."),
    "philosophy-of-language/linguistic-abstraction/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-4", heading="Choose the lowest level of abstraction that preserves the decision-relevant structure", paragraph="‘Systemic inequality’ may be appropriate in a policy overview but too compressed for a reader who needs to know which rule, population, and outcome are involved. Conversely, listing every individual event can bury the pattern the concept was meant to reveal. Test the level by asking whether the audience can paraphrase the claim, identify a concrete example, and tell what evidence could challenge it."),
    "philosophy-of-language/semantics-convention-vs-stipulation/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-5", heading="Stipulation proposes a meaning; convention emerges when a community reliably coordinates on it", paragraph="A writer may define ‘rationality’ to include emotional self-regulation, but that private decision does not change how the wider language works. If readers adopt the usage because it proves useful, the stipulation can gradually become conventional. Disputes become clearer when speakers ask whether they are reporting established usage, proposing a local definition, or campaigning for a social change in meaning."),
    "philosophy-of-language/the-linearity-of-language/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-1", heading="Sequential language forces an order that reality itself may not possess", paragraph="A sentence presents cause, context, qualification, and outcome one after another, even when those elements interact simultaneously. The order can quietly imply priority: what comes first may feel more basic, and what arrives in a footnote may seem less important. Diagrams can restore visible relationships, but prose remains better at guiding attention through an argument; strong explanation uses each medium for the structure it represents best."),
    "philosophy-of-mind/assessing-mind-with-mind/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-3", heading="Cross-checks improve reliability by making different error routes collide", paragraph="Vision and touch can jointly identify an object because their failures are not perfectly aligned; memory can be checked against records, prediction against later observation, and private calculation against another person’s method. None provides a view from outside all cognition, but agreement among partly independent processes makes a shared error less likely. The strength of the check depends on genuine independence—two systems trained on the same flawed source may only repeat one mistake."),
    "philosophy-of-mind/manufacturer-or-method/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-4", heading="Reliability is earned by a method’s performance, not guaranteed by a story about its maker", paragraph="A divine creator, evolution, simulation, or chance might explain where cognition came from, but the source alone does not show that every cognitive output is trustworthy. We assess faculties through calibration, successful prediction, error correction, independent checks, and comparison with alternatives. This inquiry is unavoidably conducted with the mind, yet it need not be empty circularity: a fallible instrument can discover and compensate for its own patterned failures."),
    "philosophy-of-mind/philosophy-of-mind-basics/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-3", heading="The field persists because several questions remain entangled but not identical", paragraph="What makes a state conscious, how mental states cause action, whether thought requires representation, and what unifies a person over time are separate problems. A theory can make progress on one while leaving the others open—for example, a functional account of memory need not settle why remembering has a felt quality. Beginners gain clarity by tracking which problem an argument addresses instead of treating ‘the mind’ as one undivided mystery."),
    "philosophy-of-mind/where-are-our-thoughts/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-1", heading="Thoughts need not appear as tiny objects in the brain to be physically realized", paragraph="A melody is not found as a miniature sound inside a music file; it is a pattern encoded in a medium and produced through an organized process. Likewise, a thought may be realized by changing neural activity without occupying one location or resembling what it represents. Failure to see a picture-like thought under a microscope therefore challenges only a crude storage metaphor, not physical explanation as such."),
    "philosophy-of-science/asymmetric-counterfactuals/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-5", heading="Counterfactual discipline requires comparing the actual outcome with several plausible alternatives", paragraph="After a failed policy, people often imagine one obvious better path; after success, they rarely ask whether an even better result was available. Both judgments suffer when alternatives are selected to flatter hindsight. A stronger review specifies the decision point, limits alternatives to information available then, traces likely consequences, and assigns ranges rather than pretending the unrealized path has a known outcome."),
    "philosophy-of-science/correlation-and-causation/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-3", heading="Causal inquiry asks what would change the outcome, not merely what travels beside it", paragraph="Begin by confirming the association, establishing temporal order, and listing common causes that could produce both variables. Then seek natural experiments, randomized interventions, dose-response patterns, mechanism evidence, and predictions that distinguish competing causal diagrams. No single clue is decisive in every field, but converging tests can turn a suggestive correlation into a warranted causal claim."),
    "philosophy-of-science/elements-of-research-design/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-1", heading="A rigorous design connects a precise question to observations capable of answering it", paragraph="The chain runs from question and hypothesis through operational definitions, sampling, comparison, measurement, analysis, and planned interpretation. Weakness anywhere can break the inference: a huge sample does not repair a biased measure, and elegant statistics do not create an adequate control group. The design should also reveal its failure conditions—missing data, alternative explanations, stopping rules, and the population to which the result may reasonably generalize."),
    "philosophy-of-science/is-history-science/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-2", heading="Claims about motives require more caution than claims about observable actions", paragraph="Documents can establish what an actor wrote, ordered, or publicly defended, but private intention may differ from all three. Historians strengthen a motive claim by combining independent correspondence, behavior under changing incentives, testimony from different positions, and consequences the actor tried to prevent or promote. Even then, layered motives are often more plausible than a single hidden purpose assigned with certainty."),
    "philosophy-of-science/research-design/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-1", heading="Different fields share an inferential skeleton even when experiments are impossible", paragraph="Physics may manipulate variables in a laboratory, epidemiology may exploit natural contrasts, and history may compare traces left by unrepeatable events. Each still needs a clear question, reliable measurement, explicit alternatives, a defensible comparison, and an account of how evidence bears on the conclusion. Research design is therefore not one universal procedure but a disciplined match between question, accessible evidence, and credible inference."),
    "philosophy-of-science/scientism-faith/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-1", heading="Scientism and faith become useful contrasts only after their scopes are specified", paragraph="Science is a family of public methods for testing claims about observable reality; it does not by itself choose every value or settle every conceptual question. Faith may mean trust, commitment, a religious practice, or belief beyond available evidence, and those meanings have different epistemic standing. Criticism should target the actual overreach—treating science as exhaustive metaphysics or treating conviction as confirmation—rather than trading slogans between undefined camps."),
    "philosophy-of-science/the-power-of-thought-experiments/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-1", heading="A thought experiment isolates commitments; it does not manufacture evidence", paragraph="By imagining a trolley switch, a duplicate Earth, or a sealed room, we can expose tensions among principles that ordinary cases keep tangled together. The result depends on what the scenario legitimately holds fixed and on whether our intuition tracks the relevant feature rather than vivid framing. Thought experiments are strongest as conceptual stress tests whose lessons are later checked against theory, empirical knowledge, and altered versions of the case."),
    "philosophy-of-science/what-is-induction/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-1", heading="Induction extends beyond the observed by betting on structured continuity", paragraph="From repeated sunrises, sampled voters, or successful treatments, we infer something about an unobserved case. The inference is not deductively guaranteed: tomorrow, the unsampled population, or a new patient may differ. Its strength comes from sample quality, stable mechanisms, background knowledge, and error-tested methods—not from repetition alone."),
    "philosophy-of-science/what-is-parsimony/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-1", heading="Parsimony prefers fewer unsupported commitments, not the shortest story", paragraph="A complicated model may be more parsimonious than a simple slogan if each of its moving parts is independently supported. Conversely, adding an invisible exception whenever a prediction fails makes a theory less economical even if its verbal description stays brief. Parsimony breaks ties among explanations with comparable fit; it should not defeat stronger evidence merely because reality turns out to be complicated."),
    "political-philosophy/peaceful-revolutions/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-1", heading="Nonviolent political change depends on leverage, restraint, and institutions—not goodwill alone", paragraph="Mass participation can withdraw labor, cooperation, legitimacy, and administrative compliance without matching the state’s weapons. Success is more likely when movements maintain discipline, split elites, attract broad coalitions, and preserve a credible path for transition. Peacefulness describes the movement’s method, not a guarantee of safety: repression can still be severe, and structural conditions shape which tactics remain possible."),
    "political-philosophy/political-philosophy-basics/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-3", heading="Political philosophy asks how coercive power can be justified among people who disagree", paragraph="Liberty, equality, authority, property, justice, democracy, and obligation are connected because public rules distribute both power and vulnerability. A theory must explain not only its preferred outcome but who may impose it, through what procedure, and with what protection for dissenters. Concrete cases help: taxation, speech limits, policing, migration, and health mandates reveal where attractive principles begin to compete."),
    "political-philosophy/political-philosophy-core-concepts/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-2", heading="The central concepts matter through their conflicts, not as isolated definitions", paragraph="Liberty can conflict with equality, authority with autonomy, security with privacy, and majority rule with minority rights. Institutions do not eliminate these tensions; they decide who bears the cost when values cannot all be maximized. A useful conceptual map therefore pairs every ideal with its rival claims, decision procedures, and safeguards against predictable abuse."),
    "rational-thought/ai-logic-intelligence/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-1", heading="Logical consistency is one component of intelligence, not its complete measure", paragraph="A system can follow a valid rule yet choose irrelevant premises, misunderstand a goal, or fail when the context shifts. Intelligence also involves learning, abstraction, planning, error correction, practical judgment, and effective action under uncertainty. Debates about whether AI is ‘really intelligent’ improve when they replace the single label with a profile of demonstrated capacities and known failure modes."),
    "rational-thought/case-2-autism/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-3", heading="The Wakefield story spread because institutional and emotional forces amplified weak evidence", paragraph="A small, methodologically inadequate report became attached to parental fear, vivid personal testimony, media balance, distrust of institutions, and organized repetition. Retraction and later studies arrived into a social environment where the claim had become part of identity and community. The case shows why correcting misinformation requires more than publishing contrary data: communicators must expose the original evidence failure while rebuilding trusted routes for uncertainty and care."),
    "rational-thought/do-rationalists-skew-neurodivergent/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-1", heading="A plausible selection effect is not yet evidence of a population-wide correlation", paragraph="Communities organized around explicit rules, intense interests, online text, and reduced reliance on social convention may feel unusually accessible to some neurodivergent people. That could change who joins, who remains visible, and who self-identifies, without showing that neurodivergence causes superior rationality. Responsible claims need representative data, careful definitions, and attention to diagnosis, gender, culture, and the large differences within both groups."),
    "rational-thought/the-professional-application-of-rationality/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-5", heading="Professional rationality includes knowing when technically correct reasoning will still fail", paragraph="A physician, engineer, analyst, or judge must reason accurately, but also communicate uncertainty, notice incentives, invite criticism, and act within ethical limits. Intelligence without restraint can optimize the wrong target; confidence without listening can prevent corrective evidence from arriving. Mature rationality is therefore social and institutional as well as individual: it builds conditions in which errors can be found before they become harm."),
    "rational-thought/what-is-futurism/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-2", heading="Forecasting estimates likely outcomes; futurism also explores choices across several possible futures", paragraph="A forecast might assign probabilities to energy demand in 2040, while a futurist builds contrasting scenarios and asks how institutions could remain robust in each. The best futurism uses forecasting rather than replacing it, distinguishes prediction from aspiration, and records assumptions that can later be checked. Its value lies less in naming one distant future than in revealing present decisions that are fragile across plausible alternatives."),
    "rational-thought/what-is-game-theory/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-2", heading="Game theory begins when my best move depends on what I expect you to do", paragraph="Two shops choosing prices, nations choosing whether to arm, and neighbors deciding whether to maintain a shared space all face interdependent choices. The payoff is not merely money; it can include safety, reputation, fairness, or future cooperation. A model clarifies incentives by specifying players, options, information, timing, and payoffs, but its conclusion is only as sound as those simplifying assumptions."),
    "rational-thought/what-is-rational-thought/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-1", heading="Rational thought coordinates several skills around beliefs and decisions", paragraph="Logic tests whether conclusions follow from premises; critical thinking tests the premises, sources, and framing; problem-solving searches for workable paths toward a goal. Rational thought uses all three while adding calibration—matching confidence to evidence—and reflection on whether the chosen goal is coherent and worth pursuing. The skills overlap, but naming their different jobs makes practice less overwhelming and diagnosis more precise."),
    "economics/a-living-wage/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-1", heading="A living wage is objective about needs only after normative choices set the standard", paragraph="Food energy, shelter, health, transport, care, and the ability to withstand routine shocks can be measured, but societies must still decide what level of participation and security a worker should be able to afford. A single global dollar figure hides local prices, public services, household composition, and unpaid care. Transparent estimates state the basket of goods, region, family type, working hours, taxes, benefits, and update schedule so disagreements can focus on assumptions rather than slogans."),
    "economics/ai-the-future-of-work/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-4", heading="Work becoming optional would remove burdens while also disrupting identity and social structure", paragraph="Employment supplies income, but it also supplies routine, status, shared projects, friendship, and a socially legible answer to what one contributes. If automation loosens the link between labor and survival, people may gain freedom while institutions lag in distributing purpose, recognition, and bargaining power. The transition’s quality will depend on whether education, civic life, care, art, and voluntary association become credible sources of belonging rather than consolation prizes for exclusion."),
    "economics/business-risks/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-4", heading="Entrepreneurial optimism is useful for action but dangerous when it erases base rates", paragraph="A founder knows details about a particular product that an industry failure rate cannot capture, yet nearly every founder can tell a similarly exceptional story. Sound planning combines the outside view—how comparable ventures usually fare—with the inside view—what is genuinely distinctive here. Pre-mortems, staged investment, cash buffers, and explicit shutdown criteria preserve ambition while limiting the cost of predictable overconfidence."),
    "economics/economic-complexity/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-1", heading="Economic predictions are difficult because people respond to the prediction and the policy", paragraph="An astronomical forecast does not change an orbit, but an inflation forecast can change prices, wages, purchases, and central-bank action. Economies also contain institutions, expectations, feedback loops, and shocks that vary across time and place. This does not make economics futile; it means reliable claims are often conditional, probabilistic, and bounded to mechanisms and contexts rather than timeless point predictions."),
    "economics/economic-entitlements/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-1", heading="Rising expectations can express progress, entitlement, or a legitimate claim to shared gains", paragraph="Wanting safer work than one’s grandparents had may reflect moral improvement rather than ingratitude; expecting every desired comfort regardless of cost is different. The distinction turns on what is owed, by whom, under which institutions, and whether benefits can be sustained without hidden burdens on others. Historical perspective should temper status anxiety without becoming an excuse to defend avoidable deprivation."),
    "economics/economic-optimism/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-4", heading="Credible optimism reports progress, distribution, remaining harm, and uncertainty together", paragraph="A falling global poverty rate can coexist with severe deprivation, regional reversals, and people whose living costs rise faster than income. Presenting only the aggregate invites complacency; presenting only the suffering hides capacities that successful policies have already demonstrated. Public communication should show the trend, its denominator, uneven gains, causal uncertainty, and the practical work required to continue it."),
    "economics/innovation-attractors/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-3", heading="Innovation hubs become self-reinforcing when talent, capital, institutions, and trust co-locate", paragraph="Skilled workers attract firms, firms attract investors and suppliers, and repeated collaboration creates knowledge that travels through people more readily than through reports. Universities, immigration rules, housing, infrastructure, and tolerance for failed experiments can strengthen or choke this loop. The same feedback creates fragility: high costs, exclusion, groupthink, or dependence on one industry can eventually push the next generation elsewhere."),
    "economics/justified-optimism/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-4", heading="Justified optimism treats progress as evidence of capacity, not a promise of continuation", paragraph="Longer lives, lower child mortality, wider literacy, and reduced extreme poverty show that coordinated human action can improve conditions. They do not guarantee that climate risk, war, institutional decay, or new technologies will resolve themselves. The rational stance combines confidence that problems can yield to effort with vigilance about reversals, unequal gains, and the maintenance work that past successes require."),
    "economics/the-poverty-line/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-4", heading="Poverty comparisons need a dashboard because no threshold captures every deprivation", paragraph="Income relative to a local line shows whether resources meet a chosen standard, while consumption, housing quality, health, education, food security, assets, debt, and exposure to shocks reveal different dimensions. Purchasing-power adjustments improve international comparison but cannot fully equalize public services or social participation. A useful assessment reports several measures and explains which policy question each can answer."),
    "economics/what-makes-economics-dismal/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-3", heading="Economic models earn credibility by clarifying conditions, ranges, and failures", paragraph="A model may explain a mechanism well without forecasting the exact date or size of an outcome, especially when policy and expectations respond to the forecast. Credibility suffers when conditional projections are sold as certainties or when failed predictions disappear without audit. It improves when economists publish assumptions, uncertainty intervals, alternative scenarios, and postmortems that distinguish bad data, bad parameters, and bad theory."),
    "epistemology/absolute-certainty/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-1", heading="Immediate pain may be indubitable as an appearance while its cause and description remain fallible", paragraph="If it seems painfully hot, one can be maximally confident that some painful experience is occurring, yet still misidentify whether the cause is injury, nerve activity, memory, or dream. Even the category and intensity may shift under attention and language. Separating certainty about seeming from certainty about external reality preserves the force of first-person awareness without letting it support more than it contains."),
    "epistemology/case-5-vanishing-probabilities/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-3", heading="Do not compare one dramatic hypothesis with every ordinary hypothesis separately", paragraph="If ten mundane explanations each receive a modest probability while ‘a ghost did it’ is kept as one undivided option, the ordinary possibilities may look individually weaker even though their combined probability dominates. Hypotheses must be partitioned at comparable levels: supernatural explanations also divide by agent, motive, mechanism, and circumstances. The proper comparison is between coherent families of alternatives, updated by evidence that actually discriminates among them."),
    "epistemology/cromwells-rule/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-2", heading="Probabilities of zero and one make learning mathematically impossible", paragraph="In Bayesian updating, evidence multiplies prior odds; no finite evidence can raise a literal zero or lower a literal one. Reserving those endpoints for logical contradictions and tautologies keeps empirical beliefs revisable. The practical lesson is not perpetual indecision: confidence may become extremely high or low, but the model should preserve a path for sufficiently surprising evidence to matter."),
    "epistemology/establishing-cognitive-reliability-1/index.html": dict(anchor="prompt-4", heading="A method can be provisionally trusted without every use of it being equally tentative", paragraph="One sense of ‘provisional’ concerns the framework: perception, memory, and logic remain open to correction in principle. Another concerns a particular conclusion, whose confidence can be very high when independent checks converge. Confusing the two makes fallibilism sound like paralysis; separating them allows decisive action while preserving a disciplined route for discovering that the underlying method or its application failed."),
}
